# ThreadSanitizer suppression file for fiberexec
#
# Two categories of false positives arise when running under TSan:
#
# 1. Boost.Fiber internal synchronization (spinlock_ttas, context init,
#    operator delete via Boost.Fiber allocators).  These are correct TTAS
#    spinlocks and reference-counted allocations, but Boost.Fiber is not
#    compiled with -fsanitize=thread in the vcpkg build, so TSan cannot see
#    the atomic acquire/release edges that establish ordering.
#
# 2. The io_uring eventfd shutdown path.  During pool teardown,
#    shutdown_cv_.notify_all() calls schedule_from_remote() which calls
#    io_uring_scheduler::notify() (::write to notify_fd_).  The
#    happens-before between that write and the subsequent ::close(notify_fd_)
#    in the destructor flows through the kernel (io_uring CQE delivery of the
#    eventfd read), which TSan cannot observe.  The ordering is correct at
#    runtime: the thread processes the wakeup CQE and exits before its
#    scheduler is destroyed; the join() in ~fiber_pool() then provides the
#    final TSan-visible edge.

# Boost.Fiber TTAS spinlock — not instrumented
race:boost::fibers::detail::spinlock_ttas::lock

# Boost.Fiber context initialisation — operator new through uninstrumented path
race:boost::fibers::context_initializer::initialize

# Boost.Fiber allocator teardown — operator delete through uninstrumented path
race:boost::fibers::detail::default_stack_allocator

# Boost.Fiber buffered_channel ring buffer — operations are ordered through
# the internal TTAS spinlock, which TSan cannot see.
race:boost::fibers::buffered_channel

# Shutdown eventfd path: notify() write and destructor close() are ordered
# through the kernel io_uring CQE mechanism, invisible to TSan.
race:fiberexec*io_uring_scheduler*notify
race:fiberexec*io_uring_scheduler*~io_uring_scheduler
